<h2>The decree under challenge</h2>

<p>The Family Court, Ranchi dissolved the parties' marriage by mutual consent under Section 13-B in Original Suit No. 457 of 2024, by order dated 17 August 2024. The wife appealed under Section 19(1) of the Family Courts Act. Her case was stark: the marriage, she said, was solemnised on 21 April 2024 — a year later than the date the trial court recorded — so the joint petition filed on 12 June 2024 could not possibly satisfy the one-year separation that Section 13-B(1) requires. She further alleged dowry demands, harassment, and that she had been made to sign papers while hospitalised. The husband countered that the parties had settled, that she had acknowledged receiving Rs 4,00,000 and her stridhan, and that the decree should stand.</p>

<h2>The Division Bench's approach</h2>

<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Maintainability first</h3><p>A consent decree ordinarily cannot be appealed — but where the existence or freedom of the consent is itself the dispute, the appeal lies.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Foundations unverified</h3><p>The Family Court never determined the actual date of marriage, although the one-year separation requirement turned on it.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Consent untested</h3><p>No proper inquiry was made into the voluntariness of the wife's consent, despite her hospitalisation plea.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Remand, not final findings</h3><p>Decree quashed; fresh inquiry ordered into the marriage date, the settlement and consent; no view expressed on the truth of the allegations.</p></div>
</div>

<p>The Bench observed that the Family Court had overlooked basic aspects of the Hindu Marriage Act by not first determining the date of marriage, and it restored the parties to their position before the decree. Both sides must appear before the Family Court within six weeks and lead evidence.</p>

<h2>Section 13-B: what the statute actually requires</h2>

<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Provision</th><th>Content</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Separation</td><td>Section 13-B(1), HMA</td><td>Living separately for one year or more before the joint petition</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Mutual agreement</td><td>Section 13-B(1), HMA</td><td>The parties have not been able to live together and have mutually agreed to dissolve the marriage</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Two motions</td><td>Section 13-B(2), HMA</td><td>Second motion between six and eighteen months after the first; the six-month interval can be waived by the court in appropriate cases</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Satisfaction of the court</td><td>Section 13-B(2) read with Section 23(1)(bb), HMA</td><td>The court must satisfy itself, after hearing the parties and making such inquiry as it thinks fit, that consent has not been obtained by force, fraud or undue influence</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Section 23(1)(bb) is the provision this judgment breathes life into. The Family Court's satisfaction about free consent is not a rubber stamp on recitals in a joint petition; it is a judicial inquiry, and where a party is young, recently married, hospitalised or otherwise vulnerable, the inquiry must be a real one. A decree granted without it is not immunised by the label "consent decree" — it carries the seed of its own reversal.</p>

<h2>Lessons for practice in Delhi and elsewhere</h2>

<p>Mutual consent divorces move quickly through Delhi's family courts, and the pressure to record settlements at the mediation stage is considerable. This judgment is a reminder that speed cannot substitute for foundations. Counsel presenting a 13-B petition should place on record proof of the marriage date and of the separation period, ensure the settlement terms are set out with receipts or bank trails for payments and stridhan, and have each party's statement recorded in terms that demonstrate comprehension and freedom of consent. Where a decree is later attacked, these records are the difference between an appeal that fails at the threshold and a remand that reopens everything.</p>

<p>For a spouse who believes consent was extracted — by coercion, concealment or exploitation of illness — the decision confirms that the decree is not the end of the road: an appeal disputing the consent is maintainable, and an unverified factual foundation (such as the marriage date) is a legitimate ground of attack.</p>

<div class="note"><p>This article discusses a Jharkhand High Court judgment for general information. Appeals against consent decrees are exceptional and fact-specific; whether a particular decree can be challenged depends on the record of the case. This is not legal advice.</p></div>
